(7305857, 1139552, 112972, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Giles:</b></div><hr/><i> what was the lighting for this allen, beautiful capture. stunning in fact </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Hi Giles,  I used 6 lights for this 2 of which are stacked up on top of 2 other in order to create 2 super tall but narrow lights for edge lighting.  I have noticed that this way it works so much better when trying to capture dancers from head to toe.  Basically the 4 lights (2 on each side) are covering her all the way from head to toe uniformly, but I have grids on them so that they don''t spill too much on the background and the floor.  Then there is one with a round grid for the background (hitting it from upper right) and 1 giant softbox to light her front (hitting her from upper left) but that one is set to low power.  Having the last light is not necessary, but it is easier to darken her side facing the camera later if you want than not having the last light but decide to brighten it which will introduce some noise ... again the last light is nothing more than a fill.  The reason you see her face and forehead lit even in the position that she is in is in fact from the tall stacked up lights that I have created which start all the way from the floor to the ceiling.</td>', 'Originally posted by Giles: what was the lighting for this allen, beautiful capture. stunning in fact   Hi Giles,  I used 6 lights for this 2 of which are stacked up on top of 2 other in order to create 2 super tall but narrow lights for edge lighting.  I have noticed that this way it works so much better when trying to capture dancers from head to toe.  Basically the 4 lights (2 on each side) are covering her all the way from head to toe uniformly, but I have grids on them so that they don''t spill too much on the background and the floor.  Then there is one with a round grid for the background (hitting it from upper right) and 1 giant softbox to light her front (hitting her from upper left) but that one is set to low power.  Having the last light is not necessary, but it is easier to darken her side facing the camera later if you want than not having the last light but decide to brighten it which will introduce some noise ... again the last light is nothing more than a fill.  The reason you see her face and forehead lit even in the position that she is in is in fact from the tall stacked up lights that I have created which start all the way from the floor to the ceiling.', '2015-01-22 20:51:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(7305858, 1139321, 38162, '<td valign="top">I totally messed up my vote on this. damn. what a stunning image - thank you for this one.  this should have been a 9 (10 if horizon line was straightened).  my bad.</td>', 'I totally messed up my vote on this. damn. what a stunning image - thank you for this one.  this should have been a 9 (10 if horizon line was straightened).  my bad.', '2015-01-22 20:52:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(7305881, 1138388, 83149, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by ArnaMarie:</b></div><hr/><i> I was surprised to see that this did not score higher in the challenge. I really like the light and the photo has nice calm and natural feel about it. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I agree, it''s a beautiful portrait.</td>', 'Originally posted by ArnaMarie: I was surprised to see that this did not score higher in the challenge. I really like the light and the photo has nice calm and natural feel about it.   I agree, it''s a beautiful portrait.', '2015-01-22 22:06:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(7305882, 1139252, 83313, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by herfotoman:</b></div><hr/><i> Do not know how you got the deep DOF at f7.1 Was it at 135 mm and cropped. </i></td></tr></table> <br/>It was cropped, but not a huge amount.  I was pretty close, the focal length recorded in my RAW file was 38mm. I''ve trained my cycling friends to not run me over.</td>', 'Originally posted by herfotoman: Do not know how you got the deep DOF at f7.1 Was it at 135 mm and cropped.  It was cropped, but not a huge amount.  I was pretty close, the focal length recorded in my RAW file was 38mm. I''ve trained my cycling friends to not run me over.', '2015-01-22 22:11:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(7305893, 1138477, 177322, '<td valign="top">The textures are wonderful - from the bark (I can almost smell its vanilla-butterscotch scent, too.) to her fur.  Her pose is relaxed, but she is clearly aware of you. Impressive image in my opinion.</td>', 'The textures are wonderful - from the bark (I can almost smell its vanilla-butterscotch scent, too.) to her fur.  Her pose is relaxed, but she is clearly aware of you. Impressive image in my opinion.', '2015-01-22 22:32:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(7305949, 1139051, 30982, '<td valign="top">Beautiful.  This one caught my attention in voting.  Very peaceful, and such lovely detail.  I love that the bird is looking out off the frame.  A very good choice for the best of 2014!</td>', 'Beautiful.  This one caught my attention in voting.  Very peaceful, and such lovely detail.  I love that the bird is looking out off the frame.  A very good choice for the best of 2014!', '2015-01-23 01:15:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(7306005, 1139321, 96574, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Alexkc:</b></div><hr/><i> What a stunning image Mark! Out of curiosity: is it staged? You know very well that being staged wouldn''t be negative for me :) </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Yep, I had a friend of mine walk through the water during the sunrise.  He hated doing it. :)<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by oldbimmercoupe:</b></div><hr/><i> I totally messed up my vote on this. damn. what a stunning image - thank you for this one.  this should have been a 9 (10 if horizon line was straightened).  my bad. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>The horizon line is straight.  It just looks off because of the angle of the clouds.<br/> <br/>Thanks for all the comments folks.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2015-01-23 08:44:20.</i></td>', 'Originally posted by Alexkc: What a stunning image Mark! Out of curiosity: is it staged? You know very well that being staged wouldn''t be negative for me :)   Yep, I had a friend of mine walk through the water during the sunrise.  He hated doing it. :) Originally posted by oldbimmercoupe: I totally messed up my vote on this. damn. what a stunning image - thank you for this one.  this should have been a 9 (10 if horizon line was straightened).  my bad.   The horizon line is straight.  It just looks off because of the angle of the clouds. Thanks for all the comments folks.', '2015-01-23 08:43:52.000000', '2015-01-23 08:44:20.000000', 0),
